## 2. IDENTIFY

### 2.A Maintain Asset Inventory

- [ ] Maintain a regularly updated inventory of IT, OT, cloud, SaaS, network, and internet-facing assets.
- [ ] Include asset owner, business function, network location, criticality, software/firmware where practical, and exposure status.
- [ ] Update the inventory on a recurring basis and after material technology or environment changes.

### 2.B Identify Critical Services and Dependencies

- [ ] Identify mission-critical business services, supporting systems, vendors, facilities, identities, data, and operational dependencies.
- [ ] Document recovery priorities, operational impact, and responsible owners for critical services.

### 2.C Identify Internet-Exposed Assets and Services

- [ ] Maintain a current view of public internet-facing systems, applications, remote access paths, APIs, and administrative interfaces.
- [ ] Remove or restrict internet exposure that is not required for business or operational needs.
- [ ] Apply compensating controls for required exposed services, including strong authentication, patching, monitoring, and access restrictions.

### 2.D Mitigate Known Exploited Vulnerabilities

- [ ] Track CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities and other high-priority vulnerabilities affecting in-scope systems.
- [ ] Patch or mitigate known exploited vulnerabilities on internet-facing and critical systems using risk-informed timelines.
- [ ] Document compensating controls when patching is not technically feasible.

### 2.E Maintain Secure Configuration Documentation

- [ ] Maintain baseline and current configuration documentation for important IT, OT, cloud, and network assets.
- [ ] Review configuration documentation on a recurring cadence and after significant changes.
- [ ] Record approved deviations from baseline configurations.

### 2.F Maintain Network Topology Documentation

- [ ] Maintain accurate network diagrams for IT, OT, cloud, remote access, and third-party connectivity.
- [ ] Identify trust boundaries, segmentation points, externally reachable services, and critical communication paths.
- [ ] Review diagrams regularly and after architecture or vendor access changes.

---

## 3. PROTECT

### 3.A Change Default Passwords

- [ ] Require default manufacturer, vendor, or installation passwords to be changed before production use.
- [ ] Document compensating controls where default credentials cannot be changed.
- [ ] Include IT, OT, network, cloud, software, firmware, and appliance credentials in scope.

### 3.B Enforce Minimum Password Strength

- [ ] Require strong passwords or passphrases, including a minimum password length of 15 or more characters where technically feasible.
- [ ] Use password managers or equivalent controls to support unique, strong credentials.
- [ ] Apply compensating controls for systems that cannot support the minimum password standard.

### 3.C Use Unique Credentials

- [ ] Ensure users, administrators, services, applications, and devices use unique credentials where technically feasible.
- [ ] Prohibit shared accounts except where formally approved, monitored, and documented.

### 3.D Revoke Credentials for Departing Personnel

- [ ] Disable accounts, badges, tokens, remote access, and privileged access by the user departure date.
- [ ] Include employees, contractors, vendors, temporary staff, service accounts, and emergency access accounts in review processes.

### 3.E Separate User and Privileged Accounts

- [ ] Require administrators to use separate standard-user and privileged accounts.
- [ ] Prohibit routine productivity, email, and web browsing from privileged accounts.
- [ ] Revalidate privileged access on a recurring basis.

### 3.F Segment Networks

- [ ] Segment user, server, administrative, guest, cloud, third-party, and OT networks based on risk and operational need.
- [ ] Deny unnecessary traffic by default and allow only approved communication paths.
- [ ] Route IT/OT communications through controlled intermediaries such as firewalls, jump hosts, bastions, or DMZs.

### 3.G Detect and Limit Failed Login Attempts

- [ ] Log unsuccessful authentication attempts for important systems and remote access paths.
- [ ] Alert on repeated failed login attempts, suspicious authentication patterns, and credential stuffing indicators.
- [ ] Lock, throttle, or otherwise protect accounts based on documented policy.

### 3.H Implement the Principles of Least Privilege

- [ ] Grant users, administrators, applications, services, and vendors only the access needed to perform approved duties.
- [ ] Review privileged, sensitive, and third-party access on a recurring cadence.
- [ ] Remove standing access when just-in-time access, approval workflows, or time-bound access can reasonably reduce risk.

### 3.I Use Phishing-Resistant Multifactor Authentication

- [ ] Implement phishing-resistant multifactor authentication where supported, prioritizing privileged users, remote access, cloud administration, and critical systems.
- [ ] Prefer FIDO, WebAuthn, certificate-based, or equivalent phishing-resistant methods where feasible.
- [ ] Document fallback MFA methods and compensating controls where phishing-resistant MFA is not feasible.

### 3.J Provide Basic Cybersecurity Training

- [ ] Provide cybersecurity awareness training for all workforce members at onboarding and at least annually.
- [ ] Include phishing, suspicious activity reporting, password hygiene, data handling, remote work, and incident reporting.

### 3.K Use Strong and Agile Encryption

- [ ] Use current, approved encryption for sensitive data in transit and at rest where supported.
- [ ] Remove or replace deprecated protocols, weak algorithms, and insecure cryptographic configurations.
- [ ] Track systems that require compensating controls or modernization due to cryptographic limitations.

### 3.L Secure Sensitive Data

- [ ] Identify sensitive data, credentials, secrets, keys, personal information, and regulated information.
- [ ] Store credentials and secrets in secure vaults or approved secret-management systems.
- [ ] Restrict plaintext storage, uncontrolled sharing, and excessive retention of sensitive data.

### 3.M Protect Email and Messaging

- [ ] Implement email authentication and transport protections such as SPF, DKIM, DMARC, and STARTTLS where applicable.
- [ ] Configure phishing, malware, impersonation, and suspicious attachment protections.
- [ ] Review exceptions and monitor spoofing or domain-abuse indicators.

### 3.N Disable Macros by Default

- [ ] Disable macros from untrusted sources by default.
- [ ] Document authorized macro exceptions and limit them to approved users, files, and business needs.

### 3.O Require Hardware and Software Approval

- [ ] Maintain an approval process for new hardware, software, cloud services, SaaS tools, firmware, and OT technology.
- [ ] Maintain an allowlist or approved inventory where feasible.
- [ ] Remove, block, or investigate unauthorized technology.

### 3.P Maintain and Test Backups

- [ ] Perform backups for critical systems, data, configurations, SaaS repositories, OT engineering artifacts, and recovery documentation.
- [ ] Store backups separately from production environments and protect them from unauthorized alteration or deletion.
- [ ] Test restoration on a recurring cadence and document results.

### 3.Q Prohibit Unauthorized Devices and Media

- [ ] Maintain policies and technical controls that restrict unauthorized hardware, removable media, and unmanaged devices.
- [ ] Disable, monitor, or physically secure ports where feasible, especially in sensitive IT and OT environments.
- [ ] Define an exception process for approved operational needs.

### 3.R Reduce Exploitable Internet-Facing Services

- [ ] Disable unnecessary services on internet-facing systems.
- [ ] Restrict exposed administrative interfaces, remote access services, and legacy protocols.
- [ ] Apply additional protections for services that must remain exposed, including MFA, patching, logging, and access controls.

